{:edb/db-before-t 1866 :edb/db-after-t 1867 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535179 50 #inst "2026-10-08T18:49:55.581Z" 13194139535179 true] [17592186124263 1100 "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/assets/b2240a11f1760050914a9c02554e0b7e86cd84d420fb437a43d95b474103b26c.png) and $A^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" 13194139535179 true] [17592186124263 1100 "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$ and $A^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q$." 13194139535179 false] [17592186124263 1145 "Let's find the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A - λI) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}-8 - λ & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(-8 - λ)(7 - λ) + 54 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + λ - 2 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{2} + A - 2I &= O\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by $A^{-1}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1}(A^{2} + A - 2I) &= O \\\\\nA + I - 2A^{-1} &= O \\\\\nA^{-1} &= \\frac{1}{2}A + \\frac{1}{2}I\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\np=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\nq=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\np+q=\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}=1\n$$" 13194139535179 false] [17592186124263 1145 "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-8-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-8-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )+54 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}+A-2I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by $A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}+A-2I) & =O \\\\ A+I-2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{1}{2}I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ $q=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and \n\n$$\np+q=\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}=1.\n$$" 13194139535179 true] [17592186124264 1100 "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\textrm{tr}(A)=-4$ and $\\textrm{det}(A)=-18$. If $A^2 = xA + yI$ for constants $x$ and $y$, then find the value of $x+y$." 13194139535179 false] [17592186124264 1100 "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\textrm{tr}(A)=-4$ and $\\textrm{det}(A)=-18.$ If $A^2 = xA + yI$ for constants $x$ and $y,$ then find the value of $x+y.$" 13194139535179 true] [17592186124264 1145 "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\np(\\lambda)=\\lambda^2-\\textrm{tr}(A)\\lambda+\\textrm{det}(A)\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(A) &= O \\\\\nA^{2} - tr(A)A + det(A)I &= O \\\\\nA^{2} &= tr(A)A - det(A)I \\\\\nA^{2} &= -4A + 18I\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx+y = -4+18 = 14\n$$" 13194139535179 false] [17592186124264 1145 "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda)=\\lambda^2-\\textrm{tr}(A)\\lambda+\\textrm{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =-4A+18I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x+y = -4+18 = 14.$" 13194139535179 true] [17592186124265 1100 "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/assets/308850190e8483ae48e8179585c01f1805e6caf92e4d4254c996bd0ffa800427.png) and $f(x) = x^{12} -x^{10} +2x-1,$ find $f(A).$" 13194139535179 true] [17592186124265 1100 "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $f(x) = x^{12} -x^{10} +2x-1$, find $f(A)$." 13194139535179 false] [17592186124265 1145 "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(λ) &= det(A - λI) \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}4 - λ & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4 - λ\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (4 - λ)(-4 - λ) + 15 \\\\\n&= λ^{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\:\\:x^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x^2-1\\:$ | $\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{x^{12} \\,-\\, x^{10} + 2x - 1 {\\phantom{\\mid}}}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | $x^{12}$ | $-$ | $x^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $2x$ | $-$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= q(x)p(x) + r(x) \\\\\n&= x^{10}(x^{2} - 1) + (2x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nq(x) = x^{10}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $A$, we have\n\n$$\nf(A) = q(A)p(A)+(2A-I)\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(A) &= q(A)p(A) + (2A - I) \\\\\n&= q(A) \\cdot O + (2A - I) \\\\\n&= 2A - I \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}8 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -8\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 false] [17592186124265 1145 "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+15 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\:\\:x^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x^2-1\\:$ | $\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{x^{12} \\, - \\, x^{10}  +  2x  -  1 {\\phantom{|}} }$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | $x^{12}$ | $-$ | $x^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $2x$ | $-$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{10}({x}^{2}-1)+(2x-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x) = x^{10}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A) = q(A)p(A)+(2A-I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(2A-I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(2A-I) \\\\  & =2A-I \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 true] [17592186124279 1100 "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/assets/cb48b48ef82e5d6cfe903488e80a8cbd48e286c864ba387450e01430e718d77c.png) find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" 13194139535179 true] [17592186124279 1100 "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$ find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$." 13194139535179 false] [17592186124279 1145 "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(λ) &= λ^{2} - tr(A)λ + det(A)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda)$, i.e., the eigenvalues of $A$, satisfy\n\n$$\n{λ_{1} + λ_{2} = tr(A); λ_{1} \\cdot λ_{2} = det(A)\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2 = \\textrm{tr}(A)= 9+(-6)=3\n$$" 13194139535179 false] [17592186124279 1145 "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda),$ i.e., the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2 = \\textrm{tr}(A)= 9+(-6)=3.\n$$" 13194139535179 true] [17592186124457 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335725 13194139535179 true] [17592186124457 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335739 13194139535179 true] [17592186124457 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335753 13194139535179 true] [17592186124457 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335809 13194139535179 true] [17592186124458 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335697 13194139535179 true] [17592186124458 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335704 13194139535179 true] [17592186124458 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335781 13194139535179 true] [17592186124458 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335788 13194139535179 true] [17592186124458 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335802 13194139535179 true] [17592186124459 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335711 13194139535179 true] [17592186124459 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335718 13194139535179 true] [17592186124459 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335746 13194139535179 true] [17592186124459 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335816 13194139535179 true] [17592186124459 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335823 13194139535179 true] [17592186124473 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335732 13194139535179 true] [17592186124473 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335760 13194139535179 true] [17592186124473 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335767 13194139535179 true] [17592186124473 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335774 13194139535179 true] [17592186124473 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335795 13194139535179 true] [17592186125013 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535179 true] [17592186125013 1100 "Given that $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$ and $A^{-1} = pI + qA$, find the value of $p + q$." 13194139535179 false] [17592186125013 1100 "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" 13194139535179 true] [17592186125013 1145 "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 1 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-8A+9I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-8A+9I) & =O \\\\ A-8I+9{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{8}{9}I-\\frac{1}{9}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{8}{9},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{9},$ and \n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{8}{9}+(-\\frac{1}{9})=\\frac{7}{9}.\n$$" 13194139535179 true] [17592186335697 1017 #edb/ref 17592186335698 13194139535179 true] [17592186335697 1089 "q-140521" 13194139535179 true] [17592186335697 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535179 true] [17592186335697 1100 "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\text{tr}(A)=3$ and $\\text{det}(A)=-7.$ Then, ${A}^{2}=$" 13194139535179 true] [17592186335697 1113 #uuid "0d3ee196-f513-5f13-b73d-fe743c26ad51" 13194139535179 true] [17592186335697 1145 "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =3A-(-7)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =3A+7I.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 true] [17592186335698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335699 13194139535179 true] [17592186335698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335700 13194139535179 true] [17592186335698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335701 13194139535179 true] [17592186335698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335702 13194139535179 true] [17592186335698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335703 13194139535179 true] [17592186335698 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535179 true] [17592186335698 1041 "selection" 13194139535179 true] [17592186335698 1104 #edb/ref 17592186335703 13194139535179 true] [17592186335698 1110 #uuid "efcec902-c1b